/**
 * @defgroup insulated_tank_reduction_calculator Insulated Tank Reduction Calculator
 * @ingroup treasure_hunt_calculators
 * @brief Calculates heat loss and annual energy cost for a hot cylindrical tank,
 *        with or without thermal insulation.
 * @details This calculator estimates how much heat a hot vertical cylindrical tank
 * loses to the surrounding air and the equivalent annual energy that must be
 * supplied to the heating system to compensate for those losses. Two cases are
 * supported:
 * - **Insulated tank** — the tank wall is wrapped in a layer of insulation; heat
 *   conducts through the tank wall and the insulation annulus before being
 *   dissipated to the air at the jacket outer surface.
 * - **Bare (uninsulated) tank** — no insulation is present; heat is dissipated
 *   directly from the tank wall outer surface.
 *
 * The calculation uses U.S. customary units throughout. Temperatures are in
 * degrees Rankine, lengths in feet, thermal conductivities in BTU/(hr·ft·°F),
 * and gravitational acceleration g = 32.174 ft/s².
 *
 * The calculation follows these steps:
 * 1. Evaluate air properties (thermal conductivity, density, kinematic viscosity,
 *    and specific heat) at the ambient temperature using fourth-order polynomial
 *    fits to tabulated data.
 * 2. Compute the thermal diffusivity of air from the polynomial-fitted properties.
 * 3. Compute the Rayleigh number from the outer surface temperature (jacket or bare
 *    tank wall), the ambient temperature, and the air properties.
 * 4. Derive the natural convection heat transfer coefficient at the outer surface
 *    using the Ra^(1/3) empirical correlation.
 * 5. Compute the overall heat transfer coefficient using a cylindrical multi-layer
 *    resistance model that accounts for the inner convective resistance, the tank
 *    wall conductive resistance, and (if insulated) the insulation conductive
 *    resistance and the outer air-film resistance.
 * 6. Compute the total heat loss as the sum of the convective–conductive and
 *    radiative components.
 * 7. Scale the heat loss by operating hours and heating system efficiency to obtain
 *    annual heat loss.
 *
 * Relevant formulas and symbol definitions are documented below.
 *
 * @heading{Air Property Polynomial Fits}
 * @copydoc insulated_tank_reduction_air_properties_formula
 *
 * @heading{Rayleigh Number (U.S. Customary)}
 * @copydoc insulated_tank_reduction_rayleigh_formula
 *
 * @heading{Natural Convection Coefficient}
 * @copydoc insulated_tank_reduction_natural_convection_formula
 *
 * @heading{Insulated Tank — Overall Heat Transfer Coefficient}
 * @copydoc insulated_tank_reduction_insulated_formula
 *
 * @heading{Bare Tank — Overall Heat Transfer Coefficient}
 * @copydoc insulated_tank_reduction_bare_formula
 *
 * @heading{Convective–Conductive Heat Loss}
 * @copydoc insulated_tank_reduction_conv_cond_formula
 *
 * @heading{Radiative Heat Loss}
 * @copydoc insulated_tank_reduction_radiation_formula
 *
 * @heading{Annual Heat Loss}
 * @copydoc insulated_tank_reduction_annual_formula
 */

/**
 * @defgroup insulated_tank_reduction_air_properties_formula Air Property Polynomial Fits
 * @ingroup insulated_tank_reduction_calculator
 * @brief Fourth-order polynomial fits for air properties as a function of temperature.
 * @details Air properties are evaluated at the ambient temperature using curve fits
 * to tabulated air data in U.S. customary units. Each property is expressed as a
 * fourth-order polynomial in temperature @math{T} (°R):
 *
 * @formula{insulated-tank-poly; p(T) = c_4 T^4 + c_3 T^3 + c_2 T^2 + c_1 T + c_0}
 *
 * The following properties are computed (with scaling applied after polynomial evaluation):
 *
 * | Property | Unit | Scaling |
 * |---|---|---|
 * | Specific heat, @math{c_p} | BTU/(lb·°F) | × 1 |
 * | Density, @math{\rho} | lb/ft³ | × 10⁻² |
 * | Kinematic viscosity, @math{\nu} | ft²/s | × 10⁻⁴ / 3600 |
 * | Thermal conductivity, @math{k} | BTU/(hr·ft·°F) | × 1 |
 *
 * Thermal diffusivity is derived from these fitted properties:
 *
 * @formula{insulated-tank-thermal-diffusivity; \alpha = \frac{k}{\rho \, c_p}}
 *
 * @subheading{Symbols}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{\alpha; Thermal diffusivity of air; \foot\squared\per\hour}
 * @symrow{k; Thermal conductivity of air; \btu\per\hour\foot\degreeFahrenheit}
 * @symrow{\rho; Density of air; \pound\per\foot\cubed}
 * @symrow{c_p; Specific heat of air; \btu\per\pound\degreeFahrenheit}
 * @endsymtable
 */

/**
 * @defgroup insulated_tank_reduction_rayleigh_formula Rayleigh Number Formula
 * @ingroup insulated_tank_reduction_calculator
 * @brief Rayleigh number for natural convection along a vertical cylinder in U.S.
 *        customary units.
 * @details The Rayleigh number characterises the strength of buoyancy-driven natural
 * convection at the tank outer surface. It combines the buoyancy force (from the
 * temperature difference between the surface and the ambient air) with the fluid's
 * resistance to motion and heat diffusion.
 *
 * @formula{insulated-tank-rayleigh;
 *   \mathrm{Ra} = \frac{g \,\beta \,\lvert T_s - T_\infty \rvert\, d^3}{\nu\,\alpha}
 * }
 *
 * @note This formula uses gravitational acceleration g = 32.174 ft/s² (U.S. customary).
 *       The thermal expansion coefficient @math{\beta} is approximated as
 *       @math{1 / T_\infty} for an ideal gas.
 *
 * @subheading{Symbols}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{\mathrm{Ra}; Rayleigh number; \unitless}
 * @symrow{g; Gravitational acceleration (32.174); \foot\per\second\squared}
 * @symrow{\beta; Volumetric thermal expansion coefficient of air (1/@math{T_\infty}); \per\degreeRankine}
 * @symrow{T_s; Outer surface temperature (jacket or bare tank wall); \degreeRankine}
 * @symrow{T_\infty; Ambient air temperature; \degreeRankine}
 * @symrow{d; Tank outer diameter; \foot}
 * @symrow{\nu; Kinematic viscosity of air at ambient temperature; \foot\squared\per\second}
 * @symrow{\alpha; Thermal diffusivity of air at ambient temperature; \foot\squared\per\second}
 * @endsymtable
 */

/**
 * @defgroup insulated_tank_reduction_natural_convection_formula Natural Convection Coefficient Formula
 * @ingroup insulated_tank_reduction_calculator
 * @brief Natural convection heat transfer coefficient for a vertical cylinder.
 * @details This empirical correlation applies to turbulent natural convection along
 * a vertical surface. It uses the cube-root dependence on Rayleigh number that is
 * characteristic of the turbulent regime.
 *
 * @formula{insulated-tank-natural-convection;
 *   h_{nat} = 0.125 \,\mathrm{Ra}^{1/3} \frac{k}{d}
 * }
 *
 * @subheading{Symbols}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{h_{nat}; Natural convection heat transfer coefficient; \btu\per\hour\foot\squared\degreeFahrenheit}
 * @symrow{\mathrm{Ra}; Rayleigh number; \unitless}
 * @symrow{k; Thermal conductivity of air at ambient temperature; \btu\per\hour\foot\degreeFahrenheit}
 * @symrow{d; Tank outer diameter; \foot}
 * @endsymtable
 */

/**
 * @defgroup insulated_tank_reduction_insulated_formula Insulated Tank — Overall Heat Transfer Coefficient
 * @ingroup insulated_tank_reduction_calculator
 * @brief Overall heat transfer coefficient for an insulated cylindrical tank.
 * @details Heat flows in series through four resistances: the inner convective
 * resistance (tank contents to tank wall), the conductive resistance of the tank
 * wall, the conductive resistance of the insulation annulus, and the outer
 * air-film resistance (natural convection at the jacket outer surface). Each
 * conductive resistance is expressed per unit tank-diameter basis, accounting
 * for the cylindrical geometry.
 *
 * @formula{insulated-tank-insulated-coeff;
 *   U_{ins} = \frac{1}
 *     {\dfrac{r_i}{r_{o,ins}}\cdot\dfrac{1}{h_{nat}}
 *      + \dfrac{r_i}{k_{ins}}\ln\!\left(\dfrac{r_{o,ins}}{r_{o,tank}}\right)
 *      + \dfrac{r_i}{k_{tank}}\ln\!\left(\dfrac{r_{o,tank}}{r_i}\right)
 *      + \dfrac{1}{h_{inner}}}
 * }
 *
 * @note The inner convection coefficient @math{h_{inner}} = 10 BTU/(hr·ft²·°F) is
 *       a standard assumption for natural convection of process fluid inside the tank.
 *
 * @subheading{Symbols}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{U_{ins}; Overall heat transfer coefficient (insulated); \btu\per\hour\foot\squared\degreeFahrenheit}
 * @symrow{r_i; Tank inner radius (@math{d/2}); \foot}
 * @symrow{r_{o,tank}; Tank outer radius (@math{r_i + t_{tank}}); \foot}
 * @symrow{r_{o,ins}; Insulation outer radius (@math{r_{o,tank} + t_{ins}}); \foot}
 * @symrow{h_{nat}; Natural convection coefficient at jacket outer surface; \btu\per\hour\foot\squared\degreeFahrenheit}
 * @symrow{k_{ins}; Thermal conductivity of insulation; \btu\per\hour\foot\degreeFahrenheit}
 * @symrow{k_{tank}; Thermal conductivity of tank wall; \btu\per\hour\foot\degreeFahrenheit}
 * @symrow{h_{inner}; Inner surface convection coefficient (10 BTU/(hr·ft²·°F)); \btu\per\hour\foot\squared\degreeFahrenheit}
 * @endsymtable
 */

/**
 * @defgroup insulated_tank_reduction_bare_formula Bare Tank — Overall Heat Transfer Coefficient
 * @ingroup insulated_tank_reduction_calculator
 * @brief Overall heat transfer coefficient for a bare (uninsulated) cylindrical tank.
 * @details Without insulation, heat flows through two resistances: the inner
 * convective resistance and the tank wall conductive resistance. The outer
 * air-film resistance acts directly on the tank wall outer surface.
 *
 * @formula{insulated-tank-bare-coeff;
 *   U_{bare} = \frac{1}
 *     {\dfrac{r_i}{r_{o,tank}}\cdot\dfrac{1}{h_{nat}}
 *      + \dfrac{r_i}{k_{tank}}\ln\!\left(\dfrac{r_{o,tank}}{r_i}\right)
 *      + \dfrac{1}{h_{inner}}}
 * }
 *
 * @subheading{Symbols}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{U_{bare}; Overall heat transfer coefficient (bare); \btu\per\hour\foot\squared\degreeFahrenheit}
 * @symrow{r_i; Tank inner radius (@math{d/2}); \foot}
 * @symrow{r_{o,tank}; Tank outer radius (@math{r_i + t_{tank}}); \foot}
 * @symrow{h_{nat}; Natural convection coefficient at tank outer surface; \btu\per\hour\foot\squared\degreeFahrenheit}
 * @symrow{k_{tank}; Thermal conductivity of tank wall; \btu\per\hour\foot\degreeFahrenheit}
 * @symrow{h_{inner}; Inner surface convection coefficient (10 BTU/(hr·ft²·°F)); \btu\per\hour\foot\squared\degreeFahrenheit}
 * @endsymtable
 */

/**
 * @defgroup insulated_tank_reduction_conv_cond_formula Convective–Conductive Heat Loss
 * @ingroup insulated_tank_reduction_calculator
 * @brief Total convective and conductive heat loss through the tank system.
 * @details The overall coefficient, lateral tank surface area, and temperature
 * difference are combined to produce the convective–conductive heat loss. The
 * result is scaled by 10⁻⁵ to produce the output unit.
 *
 * @formula{insulated-tank-conv-cond;
 *   q_{cc} = \frac{U \cdot A \cdot \Delta T}{10^5}
 * }
 *
 * @subheading{Symbols}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{q_{cc}; Convective–conductive heat loss (÷10⁵); \btu\per\hour}
 * @symrow{U; Overall heat transfer coefficient (@math{U_{ins}} or @math{U_{bare}}); \btu\per\hour\foot\squared\degreeFahrenheit}
 * @symrow{A; Lateral tank surface area (@math{\pi\,d\,H}); \foot\squared}
 * @symrow{\Delta T; Temperature difference (surface – ambient); \degreeRankine}
 * @endsymtable
 */

/**
 * @defgroup insulated_tank_reduction_radiation_formula Radiative Heat Loss
 * @ingroup insulated_tank_reduction_calculator
 * @brief Radiation heat loss from the tank outer surface using the Stefan–Boltzmann law.
 * @details Computes the radiated heat flux using the U.S. customary Stefan–Boltzmann
 * constant and the emissivity of the outermost surface (jacket or bare tank wall).
 *
 * @formula{insulated-tank-radiation;
 *   q_{rad} = \frac{\sigma\,\varepsilon\,(T_s^4 - T_\infty^4)}{10^5}
 * }
 *
 * @subheading{Symbols}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{q_{rad}; Radiative heat loss (÷10⁵); \btu\per\hour\foot\squared}
 * @symrow{\sigma; Stefan–Boltzmann constant (1.713441 × 10⁻⁹); \btu\per\hour\foot\squared\degreeRankine\tothe{4}}
 * @symrow{\varepsilon; Surface emissivity (jacket or bare tank); \unitless}
 * @symrow{T_s; Outer surface temperature (jacket or bare tank wall); \degreeRankine}
 * @symrow{T_\infty; Ambient air temperature; \degreeRankine}
 * @endsymtable
 */

/**
 * @defgroup insulated_tank_reduction_annual_formula Annual Heat Loss Formula
 * @ingroup insulated_tank_reduction_calculator
 * @brief Annual heat loss for the tank, adjusted for system efficiency.
 * @details The total instantaneous heat loss is multiplied by annual operating
 * hours and divided by a factor of 10 and the heating system efficiency to convert
 * to the annual energy equivalent the heating system must supply.
 *
 * @formula{insulated-tank-annual;
 *   Q_{annual} = \frac{q_{total} \cdot t_{op}}{10 \cdot \eta}
 * }
 *
 * @subheading{Symbols}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{Q_{annual}; Annual heat loss (÷10⁶, efficiency-adjusted); \btu}
 * @symrow{q_{total}; Total instantaneous heat loss (@math{q_{cc} + q_{rad}}, ÷10⁵); \btu\per\hour}
 * @symrow{t_{op}; Annual operating hours; \hour\per\year}
 * @symrow{\eta; Heating system efficiency (0–1); \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 */
